{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution has no conscious goal of 'truth', whereas Popperian science explicitly aims at verisimilitude.","Natural selection is blind and gradual, while scientific testing is designed, severe, and can be revolutionary.","Organisms do not propose conjectures; scientists actively construct bold theories to invite falsification."],"overturn_conditions":["If the 'severe tests' are shown to be biased against true theories systematically.","If survival correlates negatively with truth-likeness in the long run."],"shared_structure":["Selection operates by elimination of the unfit rather than confirmation of the fit.","Survival indicates resistance to specific challenges, not inherent superiority or future invincibility.","Preference for the survivor is a heuristic for finding robustness, not a prediction of eternal dominance."],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_process"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we bet on the organism that survived a plague to be robust (not immortal), we bet on the theory that survived severe tests to be close to truth (not proven).","The rationality of the preference lies in the rigor of the elimination process, not in the properties of the survivor itself."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference that the theory is true or probable, but as a non-inductive methodological rule to select the theory with the highest 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) among those not yet falsified; this survives the charge of smuggling induction only if one accepts the cost of abandoning the guarantee of future success and admitting that the preference is a bold conjecture about which theory is closer to the truth, rather than a logical derivation from past test results.","counterconditions":["If 'severity of tests' implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature (that what worked before will work again), then the mechanism collapses into induction.","If 'verisimilitude' cannot be formally defined or assessed without inductive assumptions, then the preference lacks a rational basis distinct from probability.","If the choice is merely a 'convention' without epistemic weight, it fails to explain why scientists rationally prefer one theory over another beyond arbitrary decree."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument reconstructs Popper's distinction between 'probability' (logical likelihood based on evidence) and 'corroboration' (a report on how well a theory has withstood severe tests). It posits that preferring the better-corroborated theory is rational because severe testing maximizes the chance of eliminating error, thereby leaving the surviving theory as the best current candidate for verisimilitude. This avoids induction by refusing to claim the future will resemble the past; instead, it claims we should act on the theory that has survived the most rigorous attempts to destroy it, acknowledging that it too may fail tomorrow.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}